# Industry–Function Mapping (IFM) — core ontology
#
# The layers of the model:
#   1. Sector       — where an activity happens  (SKOS rendering of ISIC Rev. 5)
#   2. Function     — what is being done         (IFM scheme, aligned to UN CBF / APQC PCF)
#   2d. Value stream— the end-to-end sequence a function sits in
#   3. Use case     — the bridge between sector and function
#   3a. Why and how far — value drivers, transformation mode
#   3b. Who         — participations, trust roles, cost and value
#   3c. Interface   — pre- and postconditions over a shared state vocabulary
#   4. Credential   — credential types, attached to a participation
#
# Layer 4 is implemented: see ifm:CredentialType, ifm:issuesCredential,
# ifm:holdsCredential, ifm:presentsCredential and ifm:verifiesCredential, and
# ../data/credential-types.csv and ../data/participation-credentials.csv.
#
# Which parts of this vocabulary are IFM's own rather than reused from an
# external standard is recorded on each class. External classifications are
# referenced, not forked: the alignment to them is carried as SKOS mapping
# relations in ../data/function-alignments.csv.
#
# SPDX-License-Identifier: CC-BY-4.0

@prefix ifm:    <https://didas-swiss.github.io/industry-function-graph/ontology#> .
@prefix owl:    <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdfs:   <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:   <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix dct:    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .
@prefix xsd:    <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

<https://didas-swiss.github.io/industry-function-graph/ontology>
    a owl:Ontology ;
    dct:title "Industry–Function Mapping ontology"@en ;
    dct:description """Vocabulary for describing use cases for verifiable
credentials against an industry sector taxonomy and a business function
taxonomy, together with the states they consume and produce, the parties that
take part, and the credential types those parties handle. The purpose is that
the same function can be recognised across sectors and the same sector compared
across functions."""@en ;
    dct:creator "Daniel Saeuberli" ;
    dct:license <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/> ;
    owl:versionInfo "0.2.0" .

################################################################
# Layer 1 — Sector
################################################################

ifm:Sector a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:label "Sector"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """An economic activity class. Sectors are not defined here:
they are a local SKOS rendering of ISIC Rev. 5 (sections, divisions, classes).
skos:notation carries the official ISIC code.

Identifiers for divisions and classes deliberately omit the section letter.
Letters move between revisions - finance went K to L, education P to Q and human
health Q to R between Rev. 4 and Rev. 5 - while the division and class numbers
hold. An identifier keyed on the letter would have to be rewritten at every
revision."""@en .

ifm:naceRev21Code a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "NACE Rev. 2.1 code"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ifm:Sector ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:comment """NACE Rev. 2.1 is the European classification derived from
ISIC Rev. 5, and its sections and divisions are reported to be identical to
ISIC's. The code is therefore recorded as a literal at those two levels only. It
is NOT asserted at class level, where NACE subdivides ISIC according to European
requirements, nor for divisions the cross-check described in ../README.md could
not cover. See ../README.md § "Provenance" for how far that relationship has
been verified from primary sources."""@en .

################################################################
# Layer 2 — Function
################################################################

ifm:BusinessFunction a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:label "Business function"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """A reusable category of work performed by an organisation,
defined independently of the sector performing it. "Identity proofing" is the
same function in banking and in healthcare; only its regulatory context differs.

A business function must not denote an industry, a department, an actor, a
technology, a legal obligation or a desired outcome. An entry that cannot be
stated without naming one of those belongs in another layer: an industry in the
sector layer, an actor in a participation, a document in the credential layer,
an outcome in the state vocabulary.

The scheme is IFM's own. It is not a fork of CBF or of the APQC PCF; the
relation to those is carried as SKOS mapping relations, so an external
classification can be corrected or replaced without touching a use case."""@en .

################################################################
# Layer 3 — Use case (the bridge)
################################################################

ifm:UseCase a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Use case"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """An intersection node: a reusable definition of a piece of
work that a given function performs inside a given sector. Use cases are where
sector-specific regulation meets a sector-neutral function.

A use case is a pattern, not an occurrence. It is deliberately not a
schema:Action, which models an action that is being or has been performed by an
agent at a time; nothing in this graph records an instance of a use case being
carried out."""@en .

ifm:appliesToSector a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "applies to sector"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ifm:UseCase ;
    rdfs:range ifm:Sector ;
    rdfs:comment """Link to the most specific ISIC concept that is actually
warranted. A use case that genuinely spans an entire section links to the
section, not to an arbitrary class inside it."""@en .

ifm:executesFunction a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "executes function"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ifm:UseCase ;
    rdfs:range ifm:BusinessFunction .

ifm:primaryFunction a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf ifm:executesFunction ;
    rdfs:label "primary function"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The one function the use case exists to perform. Exactly one per use case."@en .

ifm:sectorHasUseCase a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "sector has use case"@en ;
    owl:inverseOf ifm:appliesToSector .

ifm:functionHasUseCase a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "function has use case"@en ;
    owl:inverseOf ifm:executesFunction .

################################################################
# Layer 2d - value streams
#
# A value stream is an end-to-end sequence of functions producing an outcome.
# The concept follows ArchiMate's Value Stream element: the what rather than
# the how. The catalogue and the stage decomposition are IFM editorial models,
# not extracts from an external standard - no openly licensed catalogue of value
# streams exists; APQC publishes end-to-end maps under its own terms and the SAP
# naming is proprietary.
#
# Streams compose functions without owning them. One function appears in several
# streams, so this is a many-to-many relation and not a hierarchy.
################################################################

ifm:ValueStream a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:label "Value stream"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """An ordered sequence of functions delivering an outcome -
procure to pay, order to cash. Stages are reified rather than listed, so each
carries its position and its own label.

Each catalogue entry here is one modelled decomposition, not the universal
sequence for that stream. Organisations decompose the same stream
differently."""@en .

ifm:ValueStreamStage a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Value stream stage"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "One step of a modelled value stream: a position, a label and the function it exercises."@en .

ifm:hasStage a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "has stage"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ifm:ValueStream ;
    rdfs:range ifm:ValueStreamStage .

ifm:inValueStream a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "in value stream"@en ;
    owl:inverseOf ifm:hasStage .

ifm:position a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "position"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ifm:ValueStreamStage ;
    rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:comment """Where the stage sits in the reading order of the modelled
sequence. An ordering and presentation hint only. It does not assert temporal
exclusivity, that the stage is mandatory, that it causally depends on the
preceding one, or any process-control semantics. In practice stages overlap,
repeat and are skipped."""@en .

ifm:stageFunction a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "stage function"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ifm:ValueStreamStage ;
    rdfs:range ifm:BusinessFunction .

ifm:valueStream a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "value stream"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ifm:UseCase ;
    rdfs:range ifm:ValueStream ;
    rdfs:comment "The modelled end-to-end sequence this use case is positioned within. Optional, and a use case may sit in more than one."@en .

################################################################
# Layer 3a - why it is worth doing, and how far it changes things
#
# Sector and function say where a use case sits. They say nothing about why
# applying a verifiable credential there is worth the effort, or whether it
# improves a process that already exists or reorganises it. Both are properties
# of the use case: the same function, in the same sector, can be a paper
# substitution in one instance and a new operating model in another.
#
# Both vocabularies are IFM editorial classifications. Neither is taken from,
# nor aligned to, an external standard.
################################################################

ifm:ValueDriver a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:label "Value driver"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """What applying a verifiable credential to this use case
reduces, removes or makes possible - friction, fraud risk, disclosure,
unreachable counterparties. A use case usually has several, and they are not
ranked. An IFM editorial vocabulary."""@en .

ifm:valueDriver a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "value driver"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ifm:UseCase ;
    rdfs:range ifm:ValueDriver .

ifm:TransformationMode a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:label "Transformation mode"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """How far the process changes: digitise, optimise, redesign or
enable. Exactly one per use case. An IFM editorial classification, not an
external standard. The first two are usually justified by a business case that
counts steps removed; the last two are justified strategically, and may show a
weak business case until they work."""@en .

ifm:transformationMode a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "transformation mode"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ifm:UseCase ;
    rdfs:range ifm:TransformationMode .

ifm:ChangeMode a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Change mode"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "An IFM editorial two-way split over the transformation modes. Derived, never typed in."@en .
ifm:Run    a ifm:ChangeMode ; rdfs:label "Run"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Improving a process the organisation already has. Derived from the transformation mode."@en .
ifm:Change a ifm:ChangeMode ; rdfs:label "Change"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Reorganising a process, or enabling something previously impractical. Derived from the transformation mode."@en .

ifm:changeMode a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "change mode"@en ;
    rdfs:range ifm:ChangeMode ;
    rdfs:comment "Run or change. Derived from the transformation mode, never typed in."@en .

################################################################
# Layer 4 - credential types
#
# Credentials attach to a participation - a party in a role - so that the issuer,
# the holder and the verifier each name what they handle. A property on the use
# case itself could not express the common case where one party issues one
# credential and another checks a different one within the same use case.
#
# The four verbs mark the lifecycle stage a use case covers: issued, held,
# presented, verified. A use case may cover only part of it. `holds` marks the
# use case in which the holder comes into possession, which is the issuing one;
# a holder presenting a credential it obtained elsewhere records `presents`
# alone.
################################################################

ifm:CredentialType a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:label "Credential type"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """A kind of verifiable credential, not an instance of one, and
not a claim, a derived attribute or a business conclusion. A credential type is
recorded here only where something is actually issued as a separate artefact.

The schema itself is published by the relevant ecosystem; what is recorded here
is the wire format, the semantic model, the trust framework, the protocol
profile and the state the credential provides evidence for - each only where it
has been established."""@en .

ifm:evidences a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "evidences"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ifm:CredentialType ;
    rdfs:range ifm:State ;
    rdfs:comment """This credential type provides evidence that may support the
establishment of the state. It does not assert equivalence: possession is not
verification, and verification is not acceptance. Whether a relying party
accepts the evidence is a governance question this graph does not settle."""@en .

ifm:credentialFormat a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "credential format"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ifm:CredentialType ;
    rdfs:comment """The credential's wire format or representation: SD-JWT VC,
W3C VCDM, and so on. A version is recorded only where the ecosystem states
one."""@en .

ifm:semanticModel a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "semantic model"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ifm:CredentialType ;
    rdfs:comment """The schema or data model family that gives the credential's
claims their meaning - the European Learning Model, a UNTP credential type.
Distinct from the wire format, which says how the claims are carried, and from
the trust framework, which says who may issue them. Omitted where none has been
established."""@en .

ifm:trustFramework a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "trust framework"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ifm:CredentialType ;
    rdfs:comment """The governance framework under which issuers are authorised
and verifiers decide an issuer is credible - swiyu for the Swiss trust
infrastructure, UN/CEFACT's UN Transparency Protocol for trade. Omitted where
none has been chosen. It is not interchangeable with the format or the semantic
model: a credential can use a format without belonging to any framework."""@en .

ifm:protocolProfile a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "protocol profile"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ifm:CredentialType ;
    rdfs:comment """The issuance and presentation profile the credential is
exchanged under, where an ecosystem names one. Omitted otherwise."""@en .

ifm:issuesCredential a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "issues credential"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ifm:Participation ;
    rdfs:range ifm:CredentialType ;
    rdfs:comment "Recorded only on a participation whose trust role is issuer."@en .

ifm:holdsCredential a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "holds credential"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ifm:Participation ;
    rdfs:range ifm:CredentialType ;
    rdfs:comment """Recorded only on a participation whose trust role is holder,
and only in the use case where the holder comes into possession of the
credential."""@en .

ifm:presentsCredential a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "presents credential"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ifm:Participation ;
    rdfs:range ifm:CredentialType ;
    rdfs:comment "Recorded only on a participation whose trust role is holder."@en .

ifm:verifiesCredential a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "verifies credential"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ifm:Participation ;
    rdfs:range ifm:CredentialType ;
    rdfs:comment "Recorded only on a participation whose trust role is verifier."@en .

################################################################
# Layer 3c - states, and why use cases compose
#
# Each use case has two ends: what must already be true for it to run, and what
# is true once it has. Both are typed against a shared state vocabulary, so the
# composition can be computed - B follows A when something A leaves true is
# something B needs.
#
# Three results come from that, and none is available from the taxonomy alone:
# the chain of use cases, the states everything needs and nothing produces, and
# pairs of use cases with identical interfaces, which are likely duplicates.
################################################################

ifm:State a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:label "State"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """Something that is true, or something a party holds. States
are deliberately coarse, because their purpose is to be shared: a state used by
only one use case cannot connect anything.

States divide into evidence states and outcome states. Both compose in the same
way - the distinction is about what the state asserts, not about how it is
used."""@en .

ifm:EvidenceState a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ifm:State ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence state"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """A party is in possession of evidence - typically a credential
it holds. Possession alone says nothing about whether any relying party has
verified it or acted on it."""@en .

ifm:OutcomeState a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ifm:State ;
    rdfs:label "Outcome state"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """A business or administrative conclusion has been reached: a
relationship is open, an entitlement is established, an attribute has been
demonstrated to a relying party. An outcome state may be supported by evidence
without being equivalent to holding it."""@en .

ifm:precondition a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "precondition"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ifm:UseCase ;
    rdfs:range ifm:State ;
    rdfs:comment "What must already be true. A use case with no precondition can start unaided."@en .

ifm:postcondition a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "postcondition"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ifm:UseCase ;
    rdfs:range ifm:State ;
    rdfs:comment "What is true afterwards. Every use case must leave at least one state behind."@en .

ifm:enables a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "enables"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ifm:UseCase ;
    rdfs:range ifm:UseCase ;
    rdfs:comment """Derived: this use case produces a state that the other needs.
It is computed from the interfaces and never entered by hand, so a wrong arrow
means a wrong precondition or postcondition somewhere."""@en .

################################################################
# Layer 3b - who does what, at whose cost, and what it displaces
#
# Credential ecosystems commonly stall for a commercial reason: the cost of
# issuing falls on one party and the benefit of verifying on another. Recording
# who bears cost and who gains value lets the graph surface where that happens.
# What follows from it is a matter for judgement, not a result the graph
# computes.
################################################################

ifm:TrustRole a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:label "Trust role"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """The role a party plays in a credential exchange: issuer,
holder, verifier, relying party or trust anchor. Verifier and relying party are
distinct roles that frequently belong to the same organisation; where one
organisation plays several roles in one use case, each is recorded as its own
participation."""@en .

ifm:Participation a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Participation"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """One party playing one role in one use case. Reified so that
it can carry who pays, who benefits and which credentials that party handles; a
direct link from use case to role has nowhere to put those.

A use case may hold several participations with the same trust role - one party
in two capacities, or two parties in the same capacity - so a participation is
identified within its use case rather than by its role."""@en .

ifm:participation a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "participation"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ifm:UseCase ;
    rdfs:range ifm:Participation .

ifm:inUseCase a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "in use case"@en ;
    owl:inverseOf ifm:participation .

ifm:trustRole a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "trust role"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ifm:Participation ;
    rdfs:range ifm:TrustRole .

ifm:party a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "party"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ifm:Participation ;
    rdfs:comment "The kind of organisation or person playing the role, not a named one."@en .

ifm:bearsCost a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "bears cost"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ifm:Participation ;
    rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:comment """Whether this party carries material cost to make the use case
work, as a yes or no. It carries no magnitude and no time profile."""@en .

ifm:gainsValue a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "gains value"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ifm:Participation ;
    rdfs:comment """direct, indirect or none. An editorial judgement, and the
conclusions drawn from cost/value asymmetry depend on it."""@en .

ifm:costValueAsymmetry a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "cost/value asymmetry"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ifm:UseCase ;
    rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:comment """True when at least one participation records bearing cost
without direct value. Derived from the participations.

An editorial heuristic for locating where funding or coordination may be needed,
not an economic result. It aggregates two yes/no editorial judgements, weighs
nothing, and supports no inference about whether a use case will be adopted."""@en .

ifm:PriorEvidenceMechanism a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:label "Prior evidence mechanism"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """A mechanism by which the same assurance is obtained today
without a verifiable credential: a paper document, a PDF, a phone call to the
issuer, a register lookup, an in-person visit. Naming it is how a claim of
friction reduction becomes checkable. It is a description of the current
mechanism, not a judgement on it."""@en .

ifm:reducesRelianceOn a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "reduces reliance on"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ifm:UseCase ;
    rdfs:range ifm:PriorEvidenceMechanism ;
    rdfs:comment """The use case reduces how far the parties must rely on this
mechanism for the same assurance. It does not assert that the mechanism is
removed from the architecture, that it is inferior, or that it ceases to be
available: a register queried less often is still a register."""@en .

ifm:requiresUseCase a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "requires use case"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ifm:UseCase ;
    rdfs:range ifm:UseCase ;
    rdfs:comment """An asserted dependency, available for cases a modeller knows
before the interfaces express them. It is validated against the interfaces: if A
requires B, something B leaves true must be something A needs, otherwise either
the assertion or the states need correcting. Prefer ifm:enables, which is
derived."""@en .

################################################################
# Use case attributes
################################################################

ifm:Scope a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Classification scope"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """How widely a use case reaches across one level of the sector
classification. Derived from the sectors a use case links to, at each of the
three ISIC levels separately, because the three answer different questions and a
single binary conflates them."""@en .

ifm:CrossSection  a ifm:Scope ; rdfs:label "Cross-section"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Reaches more than one ISIC section. Derived, not hand-maintained."@en .
ifm:SingleSection a ifm:Scope ; rdfs:label "Single-section"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Confined to one ISIC section. Derived, not hand-maintained."@en .
ifm:CrossDivision  a ifm:Scope ; rdfs:label "Cross-division"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """Reaches more than one ISIC division, or links a whole section
and so spans every division under it. Derived, not hand-maintained."""@en .
ifm:SingleDivision a ifm:Scope ; rdfs:label "Single-division"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Confined to one ISIC division. Derived, not hand-maintained."@en .
ifm:CrossClass  a ifm:Scope ; rdfs:label "Cross-class"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """Reaches more than one ISIC class, or links a section or
division and so spans every class under it. Derived, not hand-maintained."""@en .
ifm:SingleClass a ifm:Scope ; rdfs:label "Single-class"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Confined to one ISIC class. Derived, not hand-maintained."@en .

ifm:sectionScope a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "section scope"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ifm:UseCase ;
    rdfs:range ifm:Scope ;
    rdfs:comment """Whether the use case stays inside one ISIC section. Note that
this is a statement about the classification, not about industries in ordinary
usage: banking and insurance are different industries inside one ISIC
section."""@en .

ifm:divisionScope a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "division scope"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ifm:UseCase ;
    rdfs:range ifm:Scope .

ifm:classScope a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "class scope"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ifm:UseCase ;
    rdfs:range ifm:Scope .

ifm:Maturity a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Maturity"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "An IFM editorial classification of how far a use case has been worked out."@en .
ifm:Exploratory a ifm:Maturity ; rdfs:label "Exploratory"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Identified and scoped; no worked flow is linked."@en .
ifm:Modelled    a ifm:Maturity ; rdfs:label "Modelled"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A worked flow or equivalent detailed specification is linked through ifm:documentedBy."@en .
ifm:Live        a ifm:Maturity ; rdfs:label "Live"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """Documented as operational in at least one production
deployment. Evidence for that is required and is linked through
ifm:deploymentEvidence."""@en .

ifm:maturity a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "maturity"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ifm:UseCase ;
    rdfs:range ifm:Maturity .

ifm:documentedBy a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "documented by"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ifm:UseCase ;
    rdfs:comment "The worked flow or detailed specification for this use case, wherever it is published."@en .

ifm:deploymentEvidence a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "deployment evidence"@en ;
    rdfs:domain ifm:UseCase ;
    rdfs:comment "Evidence that the use case is operational in a production deployment. Required for ifm:Live."@en .

################################################################
# Provenance of the taxonomy data itself
################################################################

ifm:codeStatus a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "code status"@en ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:comment """How far the notation and label of a concept have been checked
against the official publication of the source classification. One of "verified"
(checked against the published classification) or "provisional" (seeded here, to
be replaced by an official extract). It describes the concept alone. The
confidence of a mapping onto that concept is carried separately by
ifm:mappingStatus. Never silently upgrade a value: see ../README.md §
"Provenance"."""@en .

ifm:mappingStatus a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "mapping status"@en ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:comment """How a SKOS mapping between an IFM concept and an external
concept was arrived at. One of "editorial" (a modeller's judgement, with the
reasoning recorded on the row) or "verified" (taken from a published crosswalk).
Independent of the external concept's own ifm:codeStatus: a mapping onto a
verified concept can still be editorial."""@en .

ifm:schemeVersion a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "scheme version"@en ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:comment """The edition of an external classification that the concepts
carried here follow, where one is known. Where the edition could not be
confirmed against the publisher's own document, the value says so rather than
naming a release."""@en .
